This version keeps the postorder order but uses stacks instead of recursive calls. First, nodes are collected in root-right-left order. Reversing that list gives left-right-root order, so child heights are already known when each node is processed.

```javascript
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode {
 *      constructor(val = 0, left = null, right = null){
 *          this.data = val;
 *          this.left = null;
 *          this.right = null;
 *      }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    diameterOfBinaryTree(root) {
        if (root === null) {
            return 0;
        }

        const order = [];
        const stack = [root];
        const heights = new Map();
        let answer = 0;

        while (stack.length > 0) {
            const node = stack.pop();
            order.push(node);

            if (node.left !== null) {
                stack.push(node.left);
            }
            if (node.right !== null) {
                stack.push(node.right);
            }
        }

        for (let i = order.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            const node = order[i];
            const leftHeight = heights.get(node.left) || 0;
            const rightHeight = heights.get(node.right) || 0;

            answer = Math.max(answer, leftHeight + rightHeight);
            heights.set(node, 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight));
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The map stores the height already found for each child. For a chain of nodes, the result is the number of links in that chain, not the number of nodes. This also avoids recursion depth problems in JavaScript.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the traversal list, stack, and height map.

This is the slower version that separately finds the diameter of the left and right subtrees. For the current node, it also calculates the height of both sides and joins them. It is easy to follow, but the same subtree heights may be calculated many times.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
    int height(TreeNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1 + std::max(height(node->left), height(node->right));
    }

    int diameterWithRepeatedHeight(TreeNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return 0;
        }

        int left_diameter = diameterWithRepeatedHeight(node->left);
        int right_diameter = diameterWithRepeatedHeight(node->right);
        int through_node = height(node->left) + height(node->right);

        return std::max(through_node, std::max(left_diameter, right_diameter));
    }

public:
    int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode* root) {
        return diameterWithRepeatedHeight(root);
    }
};
```

For the small tree `1` with children `2` and `3`, where `2` has children `4` and `5`, the path through `1` has length `2 + 1 = 3`. The method also works when the best path is fully inside the left or right subtree.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because height is recalculated during the diameter recursion.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for recursion, with `h` as the tree height.

A tree's longest path can be found by two searches. Start from any node and find the farthest node from it. That node is one endpoint of a longest path. Search again from that endpoint, and the distance found is the diameter.

The helper treats each child link as an undirected connection by passing the previous node. The original tree is not changed.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Val int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

func farthest(node *TreeNode, parent *TreeNode) (*TreeNode, int) {
	if node == nil {
		return nil, 0
	}

	bestNode := node
	bestDistance := 0

	if node.Left != parent {
		leftNode, leftDistance := farthest(node.Left, node)
		if leftDistance+1 > bestDistance {
			bestNode = leftNode
			bestDistance = leftDistance + 1
		}
	}

	if node.Right != parent {
		rightNode, rightDistance := farthest(node.Right, node)
		if rightDistance+1 > bestDistance {
			bestNode = rightNode
			bestDistance = rightDistance + 1
		}
	}

	return bestNode, bestDistance
}

func diameterOfBinaryTree(root *TreeNode) int {
	if root == nil {
		return 0
	}

	firstEnd, _ := farthest(root, nil)
	_, diameter := farthest(firstEnd, nil)
	return diameter
}
```

For a chain `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4`, the first search can end at `4`. The second search from `4` reaches `1` with distance `3`, which is the answer. An empty tree is handled before either search starts.

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each search visits every node at most once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive stack, where `h` is the height of the tree.

I return two values from the helper: the subtree height and the best diameter found inside that subtree. This avoids a separate shared answer variable. At each node, the possible path through it is the left height plus the right height.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    private static class Result {
        int height;
        int diameter;

        Result(int height, int diameter) {
            this.height = height;
            this.diameter = diameter;
        }
    }

    private Result visit(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return new Result(0, 0);
        }

        Result left = visit(node.left);
        Result right = visit(node.right);

        int height = 1 + Math.max(left.height, right.height);
        int throughNode = left.height + right.height;
        int diameter = Math.max(throughNode, Math.max(left.diameter, right.diameter));

        return new Result(height, diameter);
    }

    public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {
        return visit(root).diameter;
    }
}
```

The null result is `(height 0, diameter 0)`, so no special setup is needed for an empty tree. A single node also gives diameter `0`, since no edge connects it to another node.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with one visit per node.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for recursive calls, plus one small result object per active call.

